data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":260,"item_id":"msg_18061323de9647b089f2d75eb10da80c","output_index":0,"content_index":0,"text":"# Practical Guide to Tending a Small Vegetable Garden Through Spring to Winter\n\n**Introduction:**\n\nStarting a small vegetable garden in the spring is a great way to start your gardening journey. As the seasons change, the garden will have different needs and challenges. This guide provides a detailed and practical approach to tending a small vegetable garden through spring, summer, autumn, and winter.\n\n---\n\n## **1. Spring: Starting the Garden**\n\n**What to Do:**\n\n1. **Plant Seeds:**\n   - Choose a variety of small vegetables that you can grow in a pot or in the garden. Consider your space and the type of soil you have.\n   - Start with a small pot (1–2 feet in diameter) to avoid overwatering and soil compaction.\n\n2. **Planting Seeds:**\n   - Plant the seeds in well-drained soil. Ensure the soil is rich in organic matter (like compost or garden soil).\n   - Plant the seeds in a pot or in the garden, making sure to plant them in the same spot.\n\n3. **Fertilizing:**\n   - Before planting, apply a balanced fertilizer (like a 10–10–10 ratio) to enrich the soil. This will help the plant grow stronger","logprobs":[]}

